Cell["Why SolutionsX?", "Section",
 CellID->2021,ExpressionUUID->"b8aa8b70-b1fa-46cd-8cf9-ef2ec1f374b5"],

Cell["Why SolutionsX? What does it do differently to xCoba?", "Text",
 CellID->2022,ExpressionUUID->"6d3fe384-f034-4ba7-9835-252fae048b51"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "The fast tensor symmetries algorithms are all in xPerm and xTensor. xCoba \
builds on them a framework for components: ",
 ButtonBox["ComponentValue",
  BaseStyle->"Link",
  Appearance->None,
  Evaluator->Automatic,
  ButtonFunction:>NotebookOpen[
    FindFile["xAct/Documentation/English/" <> "xCobaDoc.nb"]]],
 " computes a canonical value rule for a component and stores it in the ",
 ButtonBox["TensorValues",
  BaseStyle->"Link",
  Appearance->None,
  Evaluator->Automatic,
  ButtonFunction:>NotebookOpen[
    FindFile["xAct/Documentation/English/" <> "xCobaDoc.nb"]]],
 " list of that tensor. On top of this framework xCoba implements the ability \
for parallel computations through the function ",
 ButtonBox["MetricCompute",
  BaseStyle->"Link",
  Appearance->None,
  Evaluator->Automatic,
  ButtonFunction:>NotebookOpen[
    FindFile["xAct/Documentation/English/" <> "xCobaDoc.nb"]]],
 ". SolutionsX seemingly takes a step back: it bypasses ",
 ButtonBox["MetricCompute",
  BaseStyle->"Link",
  Appearance->None,
  Evaluator->Automatic,
  ButtonFunction:>NotebookOpen[
    FindFile["xAct/Documentation/English/" <> "xCobaDoc.nb"]]],
 " and drives the ",
 ButtonBox["ComponentValue",
  BaseStyle->"Link",
  Appearance->None,
  Evaluator->Automatic,
  ButtonFunction:>NotebookOpen[
    FindFile["xAct/Documentation/English/" <> "xCobaDoc.nb"]]],
 "/",
 ButtonBox["TensorValues",
  BaseStyle->"Link",
  Appearance->None,
  Evaluator->Automatic,
  ButtonFunction:>NotebookOpen[
    FindFile["xAct/Documentation/English/" <> "xCobaDoc.nb"]]],
 " layer directly. Partially, this is because the author finds this framework \
more intuitive. More importantly this allows for:"
}], "Text",
 CellID->2023,ExpressionUUID->"ebf1d22e-ad60-4281-b617-3377318eb9a4"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "More granular control over which components are computed, when and how. The \
\[OpenCurlyDoubleQuote]how\[CloseCurlyDoubleQuote] is an important addition: \
imagine a situation where you have a perturbative solution for which each \
metric component is expanded to 10th order in some small parameter \
\[Epsilon]. It would be silly to compute Christoffel, Riemann, ... directly. \
Instead one can feed into ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  TemplateBox[{
    Cell[
     TextData["Map"]], "paclet:ref/Map"},
   "RefLink",
   BaseStyle->{"InlineFormula"}]], "InlineFormula",
  FontFamily->"Source Sans Pro",ExpressionUUID->
  "c2375abe-c2c9-4413-926c-8ba91473a8d4"],
 " or ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  TemplateBox[{
    Cell[
     TextData["ParallelMap"]], "paclet:ref/ParallelMap"},
   "RefLink",
   BaseStyle->{"InlineFormula"}]], "InlineFormula",
  FontFamily->"Source Sans Pro",ExpressionUUID->
  "3403e362-9d83-45cc-8d72-769ea189e774"],
 " the function Normal[Simplify[Series[#, {\[Epsilon], 0, 10}]]] & and have \
all your tensor components be computed only to 10th order, as clearly this is \
all you care about in such a situation."
}], "Item",
 CellID->2024,ExpressionUUID->"8d9e1379-d938-4330-a454-46e814e7f507"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "The ability to compute the components of any tensor in a unified fashion. \
That includes: tensor-valued forms, tensors with spinor indices \
(\[Gamma]-matrices, the spin connection, ...), tensors which carry indices of \
any other vector bundle. Instead of developing analogues of ",
 ButtonBox["MetricCompute",
  BaseStyle->"Link",
  Appearance->None,
  Evaluator->Automatic,
  ButtonFunction:>NotebookOpen[
    FindFile["xAct/Documentation/English/" <> "xCobaDoc.nb"]]],
 " for each case one might face, ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  TemplateBox[{
    Cell[
     TextData["Compute"]], "paclet:VasilDimitrov/SolutionsX/ref/Compute", 
    "VasilDimitrov Package Symbol"},
   "PackageLink",
   BaseStyle->"InlineFormula"]], "InlineFormula",
  FontFamily->"Source Sans Pro",ExpressionUUID->
  "4eae4bf1-b030-4abd-83bb-5bbee2c9e50d"],
 " treats them uniformly."
}], "Item",
 CellID->2025,ExpressionUUID->"41fa181e-b8f1-4230-96eb-cd3eca6d7074"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "Assumptions. SolutionsX feeds ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  TemplateBox[{
    Cell[
     TextData["$Assumptions"]], "paclet:ref/$Assumptions"},
   "RefLink",
   BaseStyle->{"InlineFormula"}]], "InlineFormula",
  FontFamily->"Source Sans Pro",ExpressionUUID->
  "2aa09635-d217-43bf-97f3-768a762aefb1"],
 " into the parallel simplification; ",
 ButtonBox["MetricCompute",
  BaseStyle->"Link",
  Appearance->None,
  Evaluator->Automatic,
  ButtonFunction:>NotebookOpen[
    FindFile["xAct/Documentation/English/" <> "xCobaDoc.nb"]]],
 " simplifies assumption-blind. Where the components carry radicals \
\[LongDash] a vielbein, a spin connection \[LongDash] the record's positivity \
conditions are exactly what ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  TemplateBox[{
    Cell[
     TextData["Simplify"]], "paclet:ref/Simplify"},
   "RefLink",
   BaseStyle->{"InlineFormula"}]], "InlineFormula",
  FontFamily->"Source Sans Pro",ExpressionUUID->
  "420a3ff6-d777-497e-90b9-63928b950eb1"],
 " needs before it will cancel them, and without them the answer is correct \
but in a form the downstream physics cannot use."
}], "Item",
 CellID->2026,ExpressionUUID->"202e3c5d-a643-476e-816a-4b9564b9cacd"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "Speed. Measured like for like \[LongDash] both engines simplifying with the \
same function, in parallel, on the same nine metric-sector arrays of the two \
5d CCLP records, with the output agreeing in size to 0.015% \[LongDash] the \
clock depends on the assumptions. Simplifying assumption-blind, ",
 ButtonBox["MetricCompute",
  BaseStyle->"Link",
  Appearance->None,
  Evaluator->Automatic,
  ButtonFunction:>NotebookOpen[
    FindFile["xAct/Documentation/English/" <> "xCobaDoc.nb"]]],
 " is about 1.6 times faster. With the record's assumptions on both sides \
\[LongDash] ",
 ButtonBox["MetricCompute",
  BaseStyle->"Link",
  Appearance->None,
  Evaluator->Automatic,
  ButtonFunction:>NotebookOpen[
    FindFile["xAct/Documentation/English/" <> "xCobaDoc.nb"]]],
 " cannot ship them to its subkernels, so it had to be handed them from \
outside the package \[LongDash] ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  TemplateBox[{
    Cell[
     TextData["Compute"]], "paclet:VasilDimitrov/SolutionsX/ref/Compute", 
    "VasilDimitrov Package Symbol"},
   "PackageLink",
   BaseStyle->"InlineFormula"]], "InlineFormula",
  FontFamily->"Source Sans Pro",ExpressionUUID->
  "49e9cafd-2109-4224-b43b-52abcd6e65bd"],
 " is level on one record and 1.2 times ahead on the other. Assumptions on is \
the regime SolutionsX actually runs in, so on its own ground ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  TemplateBox[{
    Cell[
     TextData["Compute"]], "paclet:VasilDimitrov/SolutionsX/ref/Compute", 
    "VasilDimitrov Package Symbol"},
   "PackageLink",
   BaseStyle->"InlineFormula"]], "InlineFormula",
  FontFamily->"Source Sans Pro",ExpressionUUID->
  "cd1c7b8c-02fb-4452-a269-e9ded13c78fa"],
 " wins the clock too; and where the assumptions really pay, the \
radical-bearing tensors of the previous point, xCoba offers nothing to race \
against. See the companion paper for the full benchmark."
}], "Item",
 CellID->2027,ExpressionUUID->"69ece1c2-a544-49b5-aeab-634ab024bb32"]
}, Open  ]],

